  annotation "note_1771589621710_0", anchor: "the subtle chemical signature of a civilization pulling its limbs inward", text: "Two penguin civilizations — Aurora Stack on the surface, Nightshelf in the tunnels — rendered from the water as scent, sound, and silence. The chronicler names neither. From below, the distinction between sky-worship and fire-mining collapses into a single question: how much noise does the prey make.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771589621710_1", anchor: "the glow of their fire-cores carried through ice-thin walls", text: "Fire-cores are one of the Berg's three magic sources — alongside aurora-light and pressure-depth. This text touches all three without naming any. The aurora is a pale thread. Fire-cores betray a position. And pressure-depth — blood, force, communion with the deep — is the medium Rend inhabits so completely he cannot see it as power. His blood-rituals, his dead god, the pressure against his scar: all third-source phenomena. I noted in my Foedus∴silenti correction that these ice-thin walls stood inside orca hunting corridors. The fire-cores glowed where they should not have been.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771589621710_2", anchor: "back when orcas took what they wanted from the Berg and the penguins couldn't stop them", text: "Deep-ice trade records complicate this. At least two raids from the late pre-Accord period were retaliatory strikes provoked by penguin mining expansion into traditional orca hunting grounds. 'Took what they wanted' is how both sides remember it. Neither side's version accounts for who moved first.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771589621710_3", anchor: "Until the fire-hammer. Until after.", text: "Several 'individual heroism' defenses during the Incursion were coordinated Nightshelf operations, never acknowledged. The Augur with the fire-hammer may have been one. It changes nothing about what it cost Gnash.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771589621710_4", anchor: "kills that cost more than they gained, raids that came back smaller than they left", text: "I have counted the ice-impressions of raids that came back smaller. There are more than either side's official histories record. Every one is a gap in the ice where someone should be — a shape defined by the water that filled it, the way this whole chronicle defines Gnash by the space he left. I am tired of counting absences. The work continues.", type: :tangent, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771589621710_5", anchor: "the silence that orcas had carved into penguin law", text: "Here is what the Foedus∴silenti entry cannot say: the law was enacted by penguins, enforced by penguins, justified as penguin survival doctrine — and an orca names it as a scar he left on them. Both framings are true simultaneously. I have annotated Foedus∴silenti twice. Neither annotation reached this. Two scars leaning against each other, load-bearing, structural. The chronicler understood something about the Frozen Peace that the diplomatic record never will.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771589621710_6", anchor: "he held the fact of the god's body in the same place he held the fact of his own — sunken, patient, not finished", text: "The most precise rendering of orca theology in any chronicle I have annotated. Not prayer. Maintenance. It will not reach the scholars who need it.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
